    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d247">

                <pb facs="0390" n="300" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg300"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862t.01/17: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wallace</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-29T20:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 29,
                                1920—8 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received March 30—3:47
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>848. Mission. Instructions contained in your 414 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >618</hi>], March 26, 2 p.m., communicated to Millerand this morning. He
                    made two comments.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1st:</label>
                    <item>As regards your view that further occupying German territory had no
                        bearing on the matter, he wished to inquire whether you did not believe that
                        Germany should give some guarantee that the troops would be eventually
                        withdrawn from the neutral zone and also as to your views with regard to the
                        guarantee which should be required by the Allies.</item>
                    <label>2d:</label>
                    <item>Millerand stated that he had asked Foch for an opinion as to what should
                        be done with the Reichswehr troops who had taken refuge in the British zone
                        and that he had agreed with the Marshal’s opinion that, as usual in such
                        cases, these troops should be disarmed.</item>
                </list>
                <p>I replied that while you had agreed in principle with the views of the Rhineland
                    High Commission, nevertheless you thought that permission should be granted in
                    this case for the troops to return to unoccupied territory with their arms.</p>
                <p>As regards Millerand’s first comment I undertook to refer the matter to
                    Washington and to secure an answer for him as soon as possible.</p>
                <pb facs="0391" n="301" xml:id="pg301"/>
                <p>The Conference of Ambassadors met this morning and I made for the record the same
                    statement in this connection as I made to Millerand.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
